WEBVTT

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:13.380
<v Ira Glass>At first you don't really think about it, Bobby says. He got drafted to play professional baseball for the Cubs, was sent to the minor leagues like most players are. And for the first few years, he says, you don't wonder if you'll make it. You know you'll make it. For one thing, the evidence is everywhere.
<v Ira Glass>起初你真的不会多想，鲍比说。他被选中为芝加哥小熊队打职业棒球，像大多数球员一样被下放到小联盟。他说，头几年，你根本不会怀疑自己是否能成功。你知道自己一定能成功。因为证据到处都是。

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:44.040
<v Bobby Morris>You see it all the time. You're in AA or even in A-ball and somebody does get the call. We had a shortstop when I was with the Reds. His name was Travis Dawkins. His nickname's Gookie Dawkins. And Barry Larkin got hurt in the big leagues, and the phone call came into the manager's office. And all of a sudden, this buzz started taking over the clubhouse. And it was about Gookie. Gookie's going to the show. And I remember him cleaning out his locker, getting ready to get on the next flight to Cincinnati.
<v Bobby Morris>你一直都在亲眼见证。当你身处2A或者A级联盟时，总有人会接到去大联盟的电话。我还在红人队时，有个游击手叫特拉维斯·道金斯，绰号“古奇”·道金斯。后来大联盟的巴里·拉金受伤了，经理办公室接到了电话。突然间，更衣室里开始骚动起来。大家都在谈论古奇。古奇要去大联盟了。我还记得他清理储物柜，准备搭乘下一班飞往辛辛那提的飞机。

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.400
<v Ira Glass>So he's your buddy, and you're watching him clean out his locker to go.
<v Ira Glass>所以他是你的好哥们，你看着他清理储物柜准备离开。

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.520
<v Bobby Morris>Yeah, and that's happened quite a bit. Quite a bit.
<v Bobby Morris>是的，这种情况发生过很多次。很多次。

00:00:49.520 --> 00:01:03.290
<v Ira Glass>By his third year, out of the 52 guys Bobby was drafted with, only 15 were still playing ball. In his fourth year, he was traded. By his seventh year, he was sitting on the bench a lot. Then he broke his wrist and had a hard time finding a team who would pick him up.
<v Ira Glass>到第三年时，和鲍比同一批被选中的52个人里，只剩下15个还在打球。第四年，他被交易了。到第七年，他大部分时间都坐在板凳上。后来他手腕骨折，很难找到球队愿意签他。

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:24.450
<v Bobby Morris>And I think I was pretty rusty, and I was pretty discouraged. I really thought that the time might be sooner than later that I was out of the game. There were quite a bit of people that said, is this it? Can you go back? And the questions started coming, and more regularly as I continued to play.
<v Bobby Morris>我觉得自己生疏了，也感到很沮丧。我真的觉得离开这项运动的日子可能不远了。有不少人问我：“这就结束了吗？还能回去吗？”随着我继续打球，这样的问题出现得越来越频繁。

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.680
<v Ira Glass>How would people say it? Were they sheepish about it? Or they just didn't even know to be sheepish about it?
<v Ira Glass>人们是怎么问的？他们是觉得不好意思吗？还是说他们根本不知道要不好意思？

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:37.560
<v Bobby Morris>I wish they were sheepish about it, frankly. It's frustrating when people would ask. I was always very uncomfortable when people would ask me those type of questions.
<v Bobby Morris>说实话，我倒希望他们能感到不好意思。当人们问起这些问题时，真的很令人沮丧。每次被问到这类问题，我都感到非常不自在。

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:53.880
<v Ira Glass>But then his season improved. He actually wen on a kind of streak. And his whole vision of where he was heading came back to him. And that's what keeps somebody going in this situation. There had always been so much proof, you know. He'd been a high school star, an all-state and a college star, an all-Big Ten, and his brother Hal Morris was playing for the Reds.
<v Ira Glass>但后来他的赛季表现好转了。他甚至打出了一波连胜。他对自己未来去向的憧憬又回来了。这就是支撑一个人在这种境况下坚持下去的动力。毕竟，这里一直有太多的证明了，你知道的。他曾是高中明星，入选过州全明星，还是大学明星，入选过十大联盟，而且他哥哥哈尔·莫里斯当时就在为红人队效力。

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:01.060
<v Ira Glass>It's like what happens in a casino. You win enough at the time to keep you going, to keep you in the game. It feels like the big win is right around the corner.
<v Ira Glass>这就像在赌场一样。你时不时赢一点，让你能支撑下去，留在牌局里。你感觉大奖就在转角处。

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:28.610
<v Ira Glass>What got Bobby to finally quit was that that vision of his future was replaced with a different vision of who he might become. At the beginning of his 10th year, he couldn't get onto an affiliated team. He went to the independent leagues, a step down. And when he showed up for spring training and looked around at the other guys, he thought to himself, he was seeing a lot of guys who'd hung on for too long, and wondered if that's who he was becoming. And during one of their very first practice games, he suddenly found he'd decided.
<v Ira Glass>最终让鲍比决定退出的，是他对自己未来的愿景被另一种关于自己未来的形象所取代。在第10年开始时，他找不到一支附属球队。他去了独立联盟，那是降了一级。当他参加春训，环顾四周的其他球员时，他心想，他看到了太多坚持太久的人，他开始怀疑这是否就是自己即将变成的样子。在他们最初的一场练习赛中，他突然做出了决定。

00:02:28.610 --> 00:03:23.760
<v Bobby Morris>My last at-bat-- I know I've got three at-bats. I grounded out to second base, and I did know. I knew that was it, certainly. I think the moment the ball was off of my bat, I had a thought that this might be the last one. And I ran by, the throw beat me by a couple of steps, and I kept running as hard as I can through the bag. And I just kind of kept going out to the outfield a little bit. I remember thinking after, as I'm decelerating down the baseline, just thinking, well, if this is my last one, it's kind of apropos, because I gave it everything I had. I ran down to first with everything I had, and it just wasn't enough to get me there in time.
<v Bobby Morris>我最后一次打击——我知道我有三次打击机会。我打了个滚地球到二垒，我当时就知道了。我确实知道那是最后一次了。我想球离开球棒的那一刻，我就有种感觉，这可能是最后一次了。我跑垒时，传球比我快了几步，但我还是尽我所能向垒包冲刺。我稍微多跑了一段冲到了外野。我记得当时在跑向垒包减速时，心里想，嗯，如果这是最后一次，那也算恰如其分，因为我已经竭尽全力了。我拼尽全力跑向一垒，但还是没能及时赶到。

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:39.120
<v Ira Glass>For years, he'd been wrestling with this decision, and then that was that. He was past deciding. It was done, which I think is pretty much how these kinds of big decisions always go. He went to the batting cage, he hit for a while, for the last time, and then he told his coaches.
<v Ira Glass>多年来，他一直在纠结这个决定，然后一切就结束了。他不再需要纠结了。事情定了，我想这类重大的决定大抵都是如此。他去了打击练习场，打了最后一会儿，然后告诉了教练。

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:59.520
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our radio program, "Should I Stay or Should I Go?" We have three stories of people with big decisions to make, usually, like Bobby, between the practical thing, the thing that everybody expects of them, and some vision of something that they just can't give up.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ广播电台的《美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的广播节目主题是“我该留下还是离开？”我们带来了三个关于人们做出重大决定的小故事，通常像鲍比一样，在现实的选择、大家期望他们做出的选择，与他们无法放弃的梦想之间徘徊。

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:14.180
<v Ira Glass>Act One of our show, "The Karachi Kid," in which a college student has to decide whether to stay in America, where he's been going to school, or go back to Pakistan where his family is, because as everybody knows, you can't wipe on and wipe off your visa status.
<v Ira Glass>我们节目的第一幕，“卡拉奇小子”，讲述了一名大学生必须决定是留在美国继续学业，还是回到巴基斯坦的家人身边，因为众所周知，签证状态是不能随意切换的。

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:23.530
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, "Not Far From the Tree." Two software engineers at Apple Computer get fired and then simply refuse to leave.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，“离树不远”。苹果公司的两名软件工程师被解雇后，却拒绝离开公司。

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:39.600
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "Because I'm the Mommy, That's Why." A nine-year-old girl has to decide what to do when her mom runs away, runs away with her and her sister in tow. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“因为我是妈妈，所以就这样”。一个九岁的女孩必须决定在母亲带她和妹妹离家出走时该怎么办。请继续收听。

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:52.170
<v Ira Glass>Act One. "The Karachi Kid." Muhammad Kamran is an IT student at Drexel University in Philadelphia. He worked and saved for two years to get the money to come to the States, where there are all sorts of difficulties to get a visa.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。“卡拉奇小子”。穆罕默德·卡姆兰是费城德雷塞尔大学的IT专业学生。他工作并攒了两年钱才凑够钱来到美国，而获得签证存在各种各样的困难。

00:04:52.170 --> 00:05:10.660
<v Ira Glass>He's a good student, an observant Muslim, doesn't drink. When he and his American friends go to bars, he sits there with his Diet Cokes. And after being here for school all these years, he's not so sure he wants to go home to Pakistan when he graduates. But he's the eldest son. He's expected to go home and care for his parents as they get older.
<v Ira Glass>他是个好学生，是一名虔诚的穆斯林，不喝酒。当他和美国朋友去酒吧时，他总是坐在一旁喝健怡可乐。在学校待了这么多年后，他不确定毕业后是否还想回巴基斯坦。但他又是长子。按照期望，他得回家照顾年迈的父母。

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:20.100
<v Muhammad Kamran>It's not that I don't want to take care of them. It's just that I want to do both. And I'm trying to figure out, what am I going to do?
<v Muhammad Kamran>并不是我不愿意照顾他们。只是我希望能兼顾。我正在努力搞清楚，我到底该怎么办？

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:38.790
<v Ira Glass>So in the fall, when he went home for the first time in four years, he knew his family was going to do everything possible to convince him to move back to Pakistan after college-- introduce him to marriageable women, show him how modern Karachi has become. He took a tape recorder with him on the trip. His radio diary begins right before he's about to head home.
<v Ira Glass>所以秋天时，当他四年来第一次回家时，他知道家人会尽一切可能说服他在大学毕业后搬回巴基斯坦——给他介绍适婚女性，展示卡拉奇变得有多么现代化。他带了一台录音机踏上旅程。他的广播日记就在他准备回家前开始记录。

00:05:38.790 --> 00:06:09.160
<v Muhammad Kamran>The most prominent emotion that I have right now is like, oh, my God, it's been so, so long. I wonder how everything is, and how everyone is. My cousins and my brother-- my brother was 16 when I left and his voice was just breaking then. And now he's a man. He's taller than me. I'm just so excited to go there and see everything.
<v Muhammad Kamran>我此刻最强烈的感受是，天哪，真的过了太久太久了。我好奇一切怎么样了，每个人怎么样了。我的表兄弟和弟弟——我离开时弟弟才16岁，声音才刚刚变声。而现在他是个男人了，比我还高。我真的太兴奋能回去看看一切了。

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:28.910
<v Muhammad Kamran>I'm also scared. People say it's changed a lot. It's become modern and all that. A lot of multinationals have been investing. Restaurants have opened up and there are a lot of clubs and all that kind of stuff. So I'm just scared to see what's there for me now.
<v Muhammad Kamran>我也很害怕。人们说那里变了很多。变得很现代化什么的。很多跨国公司都在那里投资。开了很多餐厅，还有很多俱乐部之类的东西。所以我很害怕看到那里现在是什么样子的。

00:06:28.910 --> 00:07:13.260
<v Muhammad Kamran>When my parents sent me to college and stuff, it was understood that, once you finish college, you will come back. There are no two ways about it. And at the time, I was like, yeah, definitely. But now that I've been here, and I see so many opportunities, I want to stay here and explore what else is here. Because it's the US. The world comes here to study and work. I took so hard to get here. Why am I going back? But then-- why am I going back? Because my family, my parents.
<v Muhammad Kamran>当初父母送我上大学时，大家达成共识，只要大学一毕业，我就得回来。没有别的选择。当时我是想，是的，当然了。但现在我在这里待久了，看到了那么多机会，我想留下来探索还有什么可能。因为这是美国。全世界的人都到这里来学习和工作。我费了这么大劲才来到这里。我为什么要回去？但话又说回来，为什么要回去？因为我的家人，我的父母。

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:49.810
<v Muhammad Kamran>I just reached Karachi. Getting off the baggage claim and getting all my stuff now. The flight was great. I sat next to this guy, a Pakistani businessman who was just coming back from Germany. He was telling me that him and his cousin were debating going to college in the US, but at the last minute his dad stopped him and he did not go. But he has no regrets. He likes being in Pakistan, because of the religion and the culture and whatnot.
<v Muhammad Kamran>我刚到卡拉奇。现在正离开行李提取处拿行李。飞行很棒。我旁边坐着一个巴基斯坦商人，他刚从德国回来。他告诉我，他和表兄弟曾商量去美国读大学，但在最后一刻他父亲阻止了他，他就没去。但他一点也不后悔。他喜欢待在巴基斯坦，因为那里的宗教、文化等等。

00:07:49.810 --> 00:08:08.500
<v Muhammad Kamran>So I'm in a rickshaw right now. I just bargained to get from Center City Karachi to my house for $1. For one dollar. You've got people honking. There's no concept of courtesy or braking.
<v Muhammad Kamran>我现在坐在一辆人力车上。我刚砍价花1美元从卡拉奇市中心回到家。就一美元。到处都是按喇叭的人。这里没有礼让或刹车的概念。

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:38.190
<v Muhammad Kamran>I must admit, I used to drive the same way before. But there's no concept of lanes. In a place where the US would have two lanes, we have about one, two, three, four-- six lanes made out of two lanes' equivalent in the US. Oh, [BLEEP], there are cows crossing in the middle of the road. This is no system of driving or anything. That's about it.
<v Muhammad Kamran>我必须承认，我以前也是这样开车的。但这里根本没有车道的概念。在美国两条车道的地方，我们这里硬是挤出了五、六条车道。噢，该死，路上有牛在穿行。这根本没有任何交通系统可言。就是这样。

00:08:38.190 --> 00:09:04.590
<v Muhammad Kamran>The first three or four days, I was sick. My stomach was running. I was hot. I felt like an American. My God, I was complaining, I was bitching about it. I was like, what the hell is this? Who wants to live here? It was hot. I made it a point to have a yogurt drink every morning just to cool my body down.
<v Muhammad Kamran>前三四天，我病了。拉肚子。天气很热。我觉得自己像个美国人。天哪，我在抱怨，我在发牢骚。我说，这到底是什么鬼地方？谁想住在这里？太热了。我每天早上都特意喝一杯酸奶来降温。

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:22.390
<v Muhammad Kamran>I was very cut back the fourth day, but the fifth day, I was like, OK, this is where I grew up, so I shouldn't complain. This is where I come from. How can I start thinking that I'm a foreigner, even though I felt like one.
<v Muhammad Kamran>第四天我感到好了一些，但第五天时我想，好吧，这是我长大的地方，我不应该抱怨。这是我的根。即便我感觉像个外国人，我怎么能开始觉得自己是个外人呢？

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:32.460
<v Muhammad Kamran>So I told my dad. He definitely wants me to stay in Pakistan or Dubai and work here. So you think I should stay in Pakistan, Abu?
<v Muhammad Kamran>所以我告诉了爸爸。他确实希望我待在巴基斯坦或迪拜工作。所以你觉得我应该待在巴基斯坦吗，爸爸？

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:38.700
<v Father>Well, after 9/11, you should consider staying back here.
<v Father>嗯，911之后，你应该考虑留在这里。

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:41.410
<v Muhammad Kamran>Why after 9/11?
<v Muhammad Kamran>为什么是911之后？

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:49.450
<v Father>It's all in the press, you see. They're saying that there's discrimination against Muslims.
<v Father>新闻里都说了，你看。他们说对穆斯林有歧视。

00:09:49.450 --> 00:10:09.160
<v Muhammad Kamran>I talked to my dad about this. And he kept switching reasons. He kept saying that, why I should come back to Pakistan, because of-- the first reason he gave was discrimination against Muslims and Arabs. And then he said that I should move home because everything's going to be set up for me. I don't have to worry about a house or a car or food and all that.
<v Muhammad Kamran>我和爸爸谈过这件事。他一直在转换理由。他一直说我应该回巴基斯坦的原因，第一个理由就是对穆斯林和阿拉伯人的歧视。然后他又说我应该回家，因为一切都为我安排好了。我不用担心房子、车子或食物这些。

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:16.690
<v Father>You don't have to start fresh here. We can provide you everything. And then you just have to work.
<v Father>你不需要在这里从零开始。我们可以为你提供一切。你只要工作就行了。

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:23.580
<v Muhammad Kamran>And then he said that there would be all these opportunities for me, because all these companies are investing in Pakistan.
<v Muhammad Kamran>然后他说，我将会有很多机会，因为所有这些公司都在巴基斯坦投资。

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:25.400
<v Father>Companies like Halliburton.
<v Father>像哈里伯顿那样的公司。

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:27.730
<v Muhammad Kamran>But you know who owns Halliburton.
<v Muhammad Kamran>但你知道谁拥有哈里伯顿吗。

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:30.950
<v Father>Well, that Dick Cheney.
<v Father>嗯，那个迪克·切尼。

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:32.960
<v Muhammad Kamran>Oh, so you know he owns it.
<v Muhammad Kamran>噢，所以你知道是他拥有的。

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:40.140
<v Father>Even Bill Gates came and sent a message to our president.
<v Father>连比尔·盖茨都来了，还给我们的总统发了口信。

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:57.910
<v Muhammad Kamran>I will do some research on this and see if Microsoft is opening something in Pakistan. Not that it's funny, but the way he put it was so silly. He's just being a dad.
<v Muhammad Kamran>我会做些调查，看看微软是不是在巴基斯坦开了什么项目。虽然这并不好笑，但他说的样子太傻了。他只是个操心的爸爸。

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:08.210
<v Muhammad Kamran>So I'm on my way to pick up my friend Binti, who has been my friend for a long, long time. She now works for a multinational in Karachi.
<v Muhammad Kamran>我现在在去接朋友宾蒂的路上，她是我认识很久很久的朋友了。她现在在卡拉奇的一家跨国公司工作。

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:10.740
<v Binti>So, Kamran, how's Hani? Is she your girlfriend?
<v Binti>那么，卡姆兰，哈尼怎么样了？她是你的女朋友吗？

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:13.510
<v Muhammad Kamran>No, actually, she's one of my school friends.
<v Muhammad Kamran>不，事实上，她是我的校友。

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:21.690
<v Binti>Girls and boys can never be friends. So cut the crap. Is she your girlfriend? Would you want her to be your girlfriend?
<v Binti>女孩和男孩之间根本不可能有友谊。少废话了。她是你的女朋友吗？你想让她成为你的女朋友吗？

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:26.520
<v Muhammad Kamran>Uh, no. Why do you ask?
<v Muhammad Kamran>呃，没有。你为什么要问这个？

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.960
<v Binti>[SPEAKING ARABIC]
<v Binti>（阿拉伯语交流）

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.200
<v Muhammad Kamran>[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE] for the American citizens.
<v Muhammad Kamran>（模糊短语）给美国公民的。

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:55.950
<v Binti>You guys suck. Muslims rule, damn it. You guys just put on this act of freedom and stuff. But you guys are full of [BLEEP]. No, actually, Muslims are nice people. Give them a break. [SINGING] Give me a break. Break me off a piece of that Kit Kat bar.
<v Binti>你们真糟糕。穆斯林万岁，该死。你们这些人只会装作崇尚自由那一套。但你们根本全是鬼话。不，其实，穆斯林是好人。放过他们吧。（唱歌）放过我吧。给我一块奇巧巧克力棒。

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:58.710
<v Muhammad Kamran>You think I should come back to Pakistan.
<v Muhammad Kamran>你觉得我应该回到巴基斯坦。

